~ start
Neri: Harbor's awake. Fish don't wait for sleepy people.
- What's biting today?
	Neri: River fish are calmer inland. Out here, sardines are steady and mackerel get more interesting when conditions line up.
	=> start
- Heard anything from the boats?
	Neri: Enough rumors to fill a warehouse. Bring me a good catch and I might sort truth from dockside nonsense.
	=> start
- See you later.
	Neri: Watch the tide on your way back.
	=> END

~ friend_2
Neri: You are getting better at reading the harbor. At least you no longer look surprised when the weather changes its mind.
- High praise.

Neri: From me? Absolutely.

=> END

~ friend_4
Neri: I kept the better catch aside because I knew you would come by.
- You were expecting me?

Neri: Do not make it dramatic. I am good at patterns.

=> END

~ heart_2
Neri: Come here. Watch the water between those two boats.
Neri: See how the surface changes before the wind reaches us? My family taught me that when I was little.
Neri: I do not usually stop long enough to teach anyone else.
- Thanks for showing me.

Neri: Remember it next time the sky looks harmless.

=> END

~ friend_6
Neri: I can tell which crate you are going to check before you even reach the stall now.
- Predictable?
	Neri: Consistent. Fishermen pretend there is a difference, so you can too.
	=> END

~ friend_8
Neri: Boats coming in from the outer route have started asking whether you have been through lately.
- Should I be worried?
	Neri: Only if you dislike having a reputation for actually helping.
	=> END

~ friend_10
Neri: Harbor used to feel like a place everyone was about to leave. It does not feel that way lately.
- What changed?
	Neri: Enough people decided to come back tomorrow.
	=> END

~ heart_4
Neri: Riverside is moving supplies again. That means Harbor can finally stop rationing half the repair stock.
Neri: You keep fixing problems upstream and somehow they wash up here as good news.
- I will take that kind of current.
	Neri: Me too. We have enough of the other kind.
	=> END

~ heart_6
Neri: Watch the line where the dark water meets the bright water.
Neri: You can read a tide chart, sure. But knowing when not to trust the chart takes years.
- You are teaching me the exceptions now?
	Neri: That is what happens when someone keeps showing up.
	=> END

~ heart_8
Neri: I know every captain who exaggerates, every dockhand who forgets details, and every gull that steals from this stall.
Neri: Somehow you made it onto the short list of people I do not have to second-guess.
- That sounds exhausting.
	Neri: Trust is extremely efficient. I recommend it selectively.
	=> END

~ heart_10
Neri: Look at the docks. Full stores, open routes, boats leaving because they have work instead of because there is nothing here.
Neri: We spent so long keeping Harbor alive that I forgot it could actually grow.
- What do you want it to become?
	Neri: A place worth returning to. Seems we are already getting there.
	=> END
